data.content:
Use EF Core 7+'s **`ExecuteUpdateAsync`** and **`ExecuteDeleteAsync`** — they run directly on the database without loading entities into memory:

```csharp
// Operation 1: Bulk update — single UPDATE statement, no entities loaded
var cutoffDate = DateTime.UtcNow.AddMonths(-6);
await _db.Products
    .Where(p => p.LastSoldDate < cutoffDate)
    .ExecuteUpdateAsync(s => s.SetProperty(p => p.IsActive, false));

// Operation 2: Bulk delete — single DELETE statement, no entities loaded
var logCutoff = DateTime.UtcNow.AddYears(-1);
await _db.AuditLogs
    .Where(l => l.CreatedAt < logCutoff)
    .ExecuteDeleteAsync();
```

**Why this fixes the problem:**

| | Before (Load + Save) | After (ExecuteUpdate/Delete) |
|--|--|--|
| SQL round-trips | 2 (SELECT + UPDATE/DELETE per row) | 1 single statement |
| Memory | 500K / 2M entities tracked | Zero entities loaded |
| Change tracker | Processes every entity | Bypassed entirely |

**Key notes:**
- These execute **immediately** (no `SaveChangesAsync()` needed)
- They **bypass change tracking** — any already-loaded entities won't reflect the change
- Available in **EF Core 7+** (you're on 8, so you're good)
- For the 2M audit log delete, if you hit timeout issues, consider batching:

```csharp
int deleted;
do
{
    deleted = await _db.AuditLogs
        .Where(l => l.CreatedAt < logCutoff)
        .Take(100_000)
        .ExecuteDeleteAsync();
} while (deleted > 0);
```
